nce**.\n\nWait — actually, in Gomoku, **if you can create an open four**, your opponent must block it, or you will win next turn. But if you can create a **double threat** (e.g., two open threes, or an open four and a separate threat), that often wins.\n\nBut here, we don’t have such complexity yet.\n\nOur best move is to **extend the existing 3-in-a-row to 4-in-a-row**, preferably in a way that keeps it open-ended.\n\nOption 1: **K8** → Forms H8-I8-J8-K8 (O-O-O-O), open toward L8.  \nOption 2: **G8** → Forms G8-H8-I8-J8, same effect.\n\nBoth are symmetric. But let\'s check nearby enemy stones.\n\nBlack (X) stones are at D2, L2, O2, F4 — all far away from row 8. No immediate blocking pressure.\n\nBut consider future expansion:  \n- Beyond K8 is L8, which is currently empty. So K8 is safe and extendable.  \n- Beyond G8 is F8, also empty.\n\nSo both directions are open.\n\nHowever, **J8 is the rightmost current stone**. Extending to **K8** continues in the natural direction of play and may be less obvious to defend.\n\nBut crucially — is there a move that **creates a 4-in-a-row with an open end**, i.e., an **open four**?\n\nYes: **playing K8** gives us H8-I8-J8-K8 — four whites in a row, with K8 being the new stone, and L8 open. Similarly, G8 also creates an open four.\n\nBut wait — **we already have three stones: H8, I8, J8**. So playing **K8** makes four: H-I-J-K → that\'s four connected.\n\nTo win, we need five.\n\nNo move completes five now.\n\nBut is there a **move that creates two threats at once**?\n\nFor example, is there a square that both extends the line and starts a diagonal?\n\nCheck diagonals from H8, I8, J8:\n- From H8: upward diagonal to G7, F6, etc. — all empty.\n- From J8: downward diagonal to K9, L10 — empty.\nNo supporting stones.\n\nSo no dual-direction threat.\n\nTherefore, best move is to **build toward five** by extending the open three into an open four.\n\nSince the line is symmetric, choice of side is arbitrary.\n\nBut conventionally, **extending forward (to higher columns)** is preferred unless there\'s a tactical reason.\n\nAlso, **L2 is occupied by Black**, but that’s on row 2 — no direct interference.\n\nThus, **K8** is a strong, aggressive move building directly toward a potential five.\n\nWait — **is K8 already occupied?** Check list: Occupied = D2, L2, O2, F4, H8, I8, J8 — **K8 is free**.\n\nSimilarly, G8 is free.\n\nBut here\'s a key insight:  \nIf we play **K8**, we create a 4-in-a-row: H8-I8-J8-K8.\n\nThat is **one move away from winning at L8** — unless Black blocks it.\n\nBut since it\'s your turn, and you cannot win in one move, you must set up the **strongest possible threat**.\n\nCreating an **open four** is the strongest non-winning threat.\n\nSo **K8** or **G8** both do that.\n\nBut let’s see: after K8, the line is H-I-J-K, open at L. After G8, line is G-H-I-J, open at F.\n\nIs one more dangerous?\n\nActually, **K8 is better** because:\n- It keeps the possibility of **future extension toward L, M, etc.**\n- There is a **Black stone at F4**, near F8 — though still distant, it might suggest caution near column F.\n- No black stones near K/L/M on row 8.\n\nBut minimal difference.\n\nAlternatively — **is there a way to create a *double open three*?**\n\nNot with current position.\n\nAnother idea: **play L8**?\n\nL8 is adjacent to K8. If we play L8 now, we get J8 and L8 with gap at K8 — that’s not good (broken line).\n\nSo **not L8**.\n\nOnly **G8 or K8** are strong moves.\n\nBut wait — what if we play **K8**, forming a 4-in-a-row? That forces Black to respond by blocking at either G8 or L8 next turn, giving us initiative.\n\nSame for G8.\n\nSo both are equally strong.\n\nBut since the sequence is H-I-J, continuing to K is natural.\n\nMoreover, in many Gomoku strategies, **extending on the open end without hesitation** is key.\n\nTherefore, **K8** is the best move.\n\nBut wait — **can White win in one move?**\n\nCheck all possible five-in-a-row lines passing through existing white stones.\n